6.2 Experience principle

TENTORA minimizes required input at the beginning. It should not force users to complete a long questionnaire before they understand the services or see relevant creative work.

Budget, deadline, deliverables, and technical requirements should be requested only when needed for purchasing, matching, or execution.

7. Path A: Design Now

7.1 Purpose

Design Now is the fast self-service path for clients who want an immediate result without beginning a full creative project.

The user does not start from a blank canvas, complete a full taste test, or wait for recommendations. The user selects a ready interactive design related to the chosen service and customizes it directly.

7.2 Journey

flowchart LR
    A[Sector] --> B[Service]
    B --> C[Ready Designs]
    C --> D[Select]
    D --> E[Customize]
    E --> F[Preview]
    F --> G[Export or Purchase]

Example:

Restaurants & Cafés → Menu Design → Select a Ready Menu → Add Items and Prices → Adjust Type, Colors, Images, and Layout → Preview → Export

7.3 Ready interactive designs

Ready designs are structured creative systems rather than flat images. Depending on the service, editable properties may include:

text and content;

typefaces and type scale;

colors and brand tokens;

images and illustrations;

layout options;

motion speed and behavior;

format, dimensions, and output channel; and

logo and brand-asset placement.

Ready designs may be produced by curated TENTORA creatives, selected studios, the internal creative team, or collaborations between TENTORA and independent creatives.

The original creative remains identified and compensated according to the design's approved commercial model.

7.4 AI within Design Now

AI accelerates customization inside an existing design system. It does not need to generate the full design from an empty canvas.

Depending on the service and creator permissions, AI may:

reflow a layout according to content length;

suggest colors compatible with an uploaded logo;

remove image backgrounds;

improve product images;

generate a missing visual element when permitted;

adapt a design for multiple channels;

translate or rewrite content;

create consistent variations; and

check readability, spacing, hierarchy, and visual balance.

7.5 Moving from self-service to a creative

If deeper customization or professional development is needed, the current work can be transferred without restarting the journey.

The transfer may include:

selected ready design;

client content and uploaded assets;

saved customizations;

preferred colors, typography, and visual properties;

exported previews; and

a structured summary generated from editing activity.

flowchart LR
    A[Customize] --> B[Save]
    B --> C[Continue With a Creative]

8. Path B: Work With a Creative

8.1 Purpose

Work With a Creative is for clients who want an original, professionally developed, or multi-service creative solution.

8.2 Journey

flowchart TD
    A[Select Sector] --> B[Explore Services and Work]
    B --> C[Select One or Multiple Services]
    C --> D[Complete One Visual Taste Test]
    D --> E[Main Taste Profile]
    E --> F[Optional Service Refinement]
    F --> G[Up to Six Recommendations]
    G --> H[Select Creative or Team]
    H --> I[Manage Project]
    I --> J[Final Delivery]

8.3 Multi-service project needs

The client can add one or multiple services to My Project Needs. All selected services remain inside one project context, preventing a separate discovery and matching journey for every service.

8.4 One taste test per project

The client completes one image-based visual taste test. TENTORA converts the selections into a structured Main Taste Profile and applies it across all selected services by default.

The Main Taste Profile may include:

visual style;

color preferences;

mood and atmosphere;

materials and textures;

shape language;

level of simplicity or boldness; and

artistic direction.

If the client wants a different direction for one service, Customize This Service Direction opens a shorter, service-specific refinement instead of repeating the full test.

The taste model supports:

Layer

Purpose

Main Taste Profile

Default visual direction for the complete project.

Service Taste Direction

Optional variation for one selected service.

Additional Taste Direction

Optional alternative when comparing more than one direction for the same service.

8.5 Creative and team recommendations

TENTORA returns a maximum of six recommendation options to avoid recreating endless profile browsing.

A recommendation may represent:

one creative for one service;

one multidisciplinary creative or studio for several services; or

one compatible creative team when several specialists are required.

For multi-service projects, TENTORA prioritizes options that preserve visual consistency. When one creative cannot cover every service, the platform may recommend a coordinated team whose Creative DNA profiles align with the project and with one another.

8.6 Selection and project workspace

Each recommendation should explain:

why the option was selected;

taste compatibility;

relevant services;

sector experience;

similar portfolio work; and

services covered by the creative or team.

After selection, the project workspace may include stages, timeline, messages, references, files, feedback, revisions, approvals, responsibilities, deliverables, and delivery tracking.

9. Core Product Components

9.1 Sector-based service discovery

The selected sector structures available services and prevents irrelevant content from appearing. Every service includes curated visual examples and access to both product paths.

9.2 Ready Design System

Every ready design requires editable properties, preview states, output formats, authorship, pricing, licensing, and customization limits.

The system distinguishes between:

properties editable directly by the client;

properties adjustable with AI assistance;

changes requiring the original creative; and

changes not permitted under the license.

9.3 Taste Engine

The Taste Engine converts visual choices into a structured Taste Profile. The MVP may begin with weighted tags. Later versions may introduce image embeddings and learned preference models.

9.4 Creative DNA

Creative DNA is derived from portfolio work and structured professional information. It may include specialty, services, visual characteristics, sectors, project experience, portfolio taste attributes, multi-service capability, availability, preferred scale, location, pricing level, and Ready Designs.

This enables TENTORA to evaluate demonstrated visual identity and capability rather than relying only on job titles or keywords.

9.5 Matching Engine

The engine compares project sector, selected services, Main Taste Profile, optional service directions, Creative DNA, multi-service coverage, and team compatibility.

The MVP may use weighted tag overlap and cosine similarity. Later versions may incorporate image embeddings, behavioral signals, completed-project outcomes, and machine-learning models.

9.6 Project Workspace

The workspace connects discovery and delivery. It stores services, taste directions, recommendations, selected participants, references, files, communication, feedback, approvals, milestones, and deliverables.

9.7 Relational data requirements

The platform must support relationships between:

sectors and services;

services and ready designs;

ready designs and editable properties;

ready designs and original creatives;

clients and projects;

projects and selected services;

projects and Taste Profiles;

services and optional taste directions;

creatives, portfolios, and Creative DNA;

recommendations, teams, and members; and

workspace activity and deliverables.

10. Role of Artificial Intelligence

AI is a core intelligence and customization layer where it reduces time, improves relevance, simplifies visual decisions, or connects self-service activity with professional execution.

Capability

AI responsibility

Interactive customization

Adapt permitted design properties to client content, assets, formats, and brand inputs.

Visual taste extraction

Interpret selected images and, in later phases, uploaded references.

Taste structuring

Convert visual choices into reusable project and service directions.

Creative matching

Compare project requirements and taste data with Creative DNA.

Multi-service consistency

Identify creatives or teams able to maintain a coherent direction.

Creative handoff

Turn self-service activity into a structured brief for a professional creative.

Project assistance

Organize briefs, feedback, revisions, files, decisions, and next actions.

Continuous improvement

Learn from permitted signals such as selections, edits, rejected options, feedback, and completed outcomes.

AI supports the product, but final artistic judgment, original authorship, professional craft, and custom execution remain human when a creative is involved.

11. Human Creativity, Ownership, and Rights

TENTORA must create value for creatives rather than use their work as unprotected input.

Every Ready Design must define:

original creator attribution;

ownership of the source design;

permitted customization;

allowed AI-assisted changes;

usage license;

whether it is sold once or licensed repeatedly;

creator compensation or revenue share; and

whether further changes require the original creative.

The platform must not use portfolios or Ready Designs for model training without clear permission and appropriate terms.

Creatives may generate revenue through Ready Design licenses, paid customization, original commissioned work, and multidisciplinary projects.

12. Unique Value Proposition

TENTORA transforms fragmented creative discovery into two connected outcomes: an immediate customizable result or an original professionally executed solution.

Its value is based on six principles:

Begin with the project sector and service, not an open profile directory.

Explain services through real creative work.

Give urgent clients a fast Ready Design path.

Use one Main Taste Profile across one or multiple services.

Limit matching to a small, relevant set of creatives or teams.

Move from self-service to professional execution without restarting.

